# Manifest gates every storage write (R2)

The AIC PIM workspace uses **`pim-app-config`** as the single
abstraction over per-binary settings storage (registry on Windows,
`/etc/aic/<program>/settings.json` on Linux, plist on macOS, the
OS keyring for secrets). Every program ships a static
**`app-config.toml`** manifest enumerating the keys it understands,
their types, defaults, secret flags, and validation rules. The
manifest exists so that:

- The configurator GUIs can render typed input widgets without
 hard-coding the schema.
- The CLI can refuse `set FOO=…` for keys the program does not
 read, instead of silently writing a value the daemon will ignore.
- Orphan keys from old releases can be enumerated and purged on
 upgrade.

The corrective audit (post-2026-05-19 win32 configurator audit)
found that `set_value` / `unset_value` did not consistently refuse
unknown keys — meaning an operator could `pim-offline-agent-configurator-cli
set TYPO_KEY=value`, see "ok", and never notice the daemon never
read it. This rule closes that gap and pins the contract for the
future.

## The rule

> **`pim_app_config::set_value` and `pim_app_config::unset_value`
> MUST refuse a key that is not declared in the active manifest,
> returning a typed `AppConfigError::UnknownKey { key, manifest }`.
> CLI `set` / `unset` subcommands surface that error with a
> non-zero exit code and a US-English diagnostic naming the
> manifest. The `pim-app-config orphans` subcommand (or its
> per-binary embedded equivalent) lists / purges keys present in
> storage but absent from the manifest.**

The library `get_value` is unaffected — reads return the stored
value if any, regardless of manifest. Reads remain permissive so an
older daemon can still load a newer settings file without crashing.

## Required behavior — library surface

```rust
// pim-app-config/crates/pim-app-config/src/app_config.rs
pub enum AppConfigError {
    UnknownKey { key: String, manifest: String },
    InvalidValue { key: String, reason: String, manifest: String },
    Storage(#[from] StorageError),
    // ...
}

impl AppConfig {
    /// Writes `value` for `key` in the configured backend.
    /// Returns AppConfigError::UnknownKey if the active manifest
    /// does not declare `key`.
    pub fn set_value(&self, key: &str, value: &str) -> Result<(), AppConfigError> {
        let entry = self.manifest.entry(key).ok_or_else(|| {
            AppConfigError::UnknownKey {
                key: key.to_string(),
                manifest: self.manifest.program_id().to_string(),
            }
        })?;
        entry.validate(value)
            .map_err(|reason| AppConfigError::InvalidValue {
                key: key.to_string(),
                reason,
                manifest: self.manifest.program_id().to_string(),
            })?;
        self.backend.put(key, value, entry.is_secret())?;
        Ok(())
    }

    pub fn unset_value(&self, key: &str) -> Result<(), AppConfigError> {
        self.manifest.entry(key).ok_or_else(|| {
            AppConfigError::UnknownKey {
                key: key.to_string(),
                manifest: self.manifest.program_id().to_string(),
            }
        })?;
        self.backend.remove(key)?;
        Ok(())
    }
}
```

## Required behavior — CLI surface

`pim-app-config set` (and every per-binary embedded clone:
`pim-offline-agent-configurator-cli`,
`pim-offline-server-configurator`) MUST exit non-zero on
`UnknownKey` and `InvalidValue`. Stdout is empty on error;
stderr carries the operator-facing message in US English. No
`println!("ok")` after a write that returned `Err`.

```text
$ pim-offline-agent-configurator-cli set TYPO_KEY=value
error: unknown configuration key 'TYPO_KEY' for program 'pim-offline-agent'

The active manifest does not declare this key. Did you mean
DISPLAY_NAME, AGENT_UUID, or HTTP_PROXY_URL?

See `pim-offline-agent-configurator-cli list` for the full key set.
$ echo $LASTEXITCODE
2
```

Exit codes (pinned for SIEM / scripting):

| Code | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| `0` | Success |
| `2` | `UnknownKey` (manifest does not declare the requested key) |
| `3` | `InvalidValue` (manifest declares it but the value fails validation) |
| `4` | `Storage` (backend write / read failure — registry locked, file permission, keyring offline) |
| `64..=78` | Reserved per `sysexits.h` for usage / config / data errors at the CLI argument level |

## Required behavior — orphan purge subcommand

Manifests evolve. A v1.2 release of the agent may drop a key that
v1.1 wrote into the operator's settings store. Those rows must be
discoverable and purgeable.

```text
pim-app-config orphans [--program <id>] [--purge] [--json]

  Lists keys present in the backend for <program> (default: the
  embedded manifest's program_id) that are not declared in the
  active manifest. Without --purge, reports only. With --purge,
  removes each orphan after a confirmation prompt unless --yes
  is also given. With --json, machine-readable output for
  upgrade scripts.

Exit codes:
  0  no orphans found (or all purged after confirmation)
  5  orphans found, no --purge requested (informational)
  6  --purge requested but at least one removal failed
```

Per-binary configurators MUST embed this subcommand under the
same name (`pim-offline-agent-configurator-cli orphans …`) so an
MSI upgrade script can call it without depending on a separate
`pim-app-config` binary being installed.

Secrets-marked orphans (the manifest entry was `secret = true`)
are purged from the **secret backend** (keyring / encrypted HKLM
sub-key); the operator-visible report shows
`[secret len=N] (purged)` so we never echo the secret value.

## Forbidden constructs

In `pim-app-config`, every per-binary configurator CLI, and every
configurator GUI that wraps the library:

- `self.backend.put(key, value)` without a prior
 `self.manifest.entry(key).ok_or_else(…)?` check — banned.
 Always go through `AppConfig::set_value`.
- `let _ = config.set_value(key, value);` — banned. Discarding
 the `Result` discards the `UnknownKey` rejection that is the
 whole point of the rule.
- `println!("ok")` / `dialog_show_success(...)` on a CLI / GUI
 path that has not first matched on the `Result` — banned.
- A `set` / `unset` CLI subcommand that returns exit code 0 on
 `UnknownKey` — banned. The shell-script call site cannot tell
 success from typo.
- A configurator GUI "Save" button that catches `UnknownKey` and
 silently drops it — banned. Render the error in the dialog's
 status pane, leave the field highlighted, do not close the
 dialog.

